     You might be able to rationalize it that way, but I'm
reasonably sure that it came about by accident.  Originally the
only way to get the protection involved was to have monsters
be afraid of moving to a spot which contained a scroll of scare
monster.  So the code added an optimization to skip the scary
check for any location--other than the hero's own spot, since
attacking is handled differently from simply moving--which
didn't have at least one object present.  Then when engraving
was eventually introduced, that optimization wasn't removed to
accomodate the fact that having an object in place was no longer
necessary.  (If keeping the object requirement was deliberate,
someone would have added a comment to the code explaining the
decision.)


